{:edb/db-before-t 2682 :edb/db-after-t 2683 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535995 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:31:53.662Z" 13194139535995 true] [17592186161941 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378669 13194139535995 true] [17592186161941 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378676 13194139535995 true] [17592186161942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378683 13194139535995 true] [17592186161942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378690 13194139535995 true] [17592186162564 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535995 true] [17592186162564 1100 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 9$ and $det(A) = 18$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is positive-definite\n3. $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139535995 false] [17592186162564 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139535995 true] [17592186162564 1145 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378669 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378670 13194139535995 true] [17592186378669 1089 "q-139455" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378669 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535995 true] [17592186378669 1100 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378669 1113 #uuid "2740a7bc-29ec-59db-9a27-d2624fe43e73" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378669 1145 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378671 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378672 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378673 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378674 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378675 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1041 "selection" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378672 13194139535995 true] [17592186378670 1110 #uuid "8fb417ec-325d-5a5e-b1eb-bd96dcdb7227" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378671 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378671 1111 #uuid "016f439d-0e77-53a1-b664-e46dc5c208c5" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378672 1034 "I only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378672 1111 #uuid "e7adc5a2-f6f9-5755-bd06-40c0738175e3" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378673 1034 "III only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378673 1111 #uuid "28abdfdc-6c7c-5e3f-8fd8-0e0d5b4f6c3b" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378674 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378674 1111 #uuid "7d5182e1-87d9-5870-a043-3c3d10fb685c" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378675 1034 "II only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378675 1111 #uuid "65d48205-bfad-5bd7-8c0a-8400bff6f2d0" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378676 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378677 13194139535995 true] [17592186378676 1089 "q-94787" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378676 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535995 true] [17592186378676 1100 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive-definite?\n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378676 1113 #uuid "f3181c19-59b2-5d98-9e5a-bcb9394e8d14" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378676 1145 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive (${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378678 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378679 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378680 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378681 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378682 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1041 "selection" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378681 13194139535995 true] [17592186378677 1110 #uuid "49057127-5159-571a-878b-6a29fb6c523a" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378678 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378678 1111 #uuid "09ded4c3-a7ef-57a4-a546-3ce37b8f027a" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378679 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378679 1111 #uuid "49f218fa-6d07-5452-b122-db9d6d481e4a" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378680 1034 "I only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378680 1111 #uuid "92e3cb8a-b05a-54a7-afe8-addb1b7e1a9b" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378681 1034 "III only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378681 1111 #uuid "f0189bab-e3b1-57cd-be82-4ad85abc68b4" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378682 1034 "II only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378682 1111 #uuid "680362f8-bfe6-52b4-a9e7-e272e175df85" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378683 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378684 13194139535995 true] [17592186378683 1089 "q-94804" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378683 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535995 true] [17592186378683 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378683 1113 #uuid "4977c7be-4118-5693-9570-927ce21d13e5" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378683 1145 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378685 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378686 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378687 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378688 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378689 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1041 "selection" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378686 13194139535995 true] [17592186378684 1110 #uuid "7cb74d44-9a29-5481-a8a0-862425b4e31b" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378685 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378685 1111 #uuid "a050a6b1-b862-500f-9c89-67caf6fe2940" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378686 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535995 true] [17592186378686 1111 #uuid "f67023bc-d0e6-5a86-8a87-5861e47891f0" 13194139535995 true] [17592186378687 